6 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186203908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049836 #edb/ref 1031 "Continuous Infinite Sets With Equal Cardinality" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049836 #edb/ref 1033 4860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049836 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "17a77d52-e3da-50bb-8685-f114fd2d124f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1031 "The Nth Term of a Geometric Sequence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1033 680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "2a8497f4-0ed3-5e71-a11d-456426f348f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050146 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050146 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050146 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186171919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050146 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186171920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050146 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186171921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050146 #edb/ref 1031 "Discrete Infinite Sets With Equal Cardinality" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050146 #edb/ref 1033 3424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050146 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "30c306d4-7422-557a-a542-cedff2b28c58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1031 "The Nth Term of an Arithmetic Sequence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1033 668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "32ad96da-6870-504d-ad58-7d14df8bea1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1031 "Cardinality of Finite Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1033 48 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050254 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "39e988d3-6f10-5718-8fbd-1ad04a159a54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171718 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171718 #edb/ref 1071 "Do the sets below have the same cardinality, and why?\n\n$$\nA=\\{ 4n \\mid \\: n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\}, \\qquad B=\\{ 2, 4,  \\ldots, 20 \\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171718 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "23214c12-0d2f-54f1-8d27-d6a70406ff58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171718 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinalities of two sets cannot be equal if one set is finite and the other is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, notice that \n\n- $A=\\{ 0, \\pm 4, \\pm 8, \\ldots \\}$ is infinite, while\n\n- $B$ is finite with  $|B| = 10.$\n\nTherefore, $|A| \\neq |B|.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\nSince $A$ is infinite and $B$ is finite, we have that $|A| \\neq |B|$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171719 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13180" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171719 #edb/ref 1071 "Do the following sets have the same cardinality? If so, why?\n\n$$\nA= \\{ -7, -2, 3, 8, \\ldots  \\}, \\qquad B = \\mathbb{N}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171719 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f394047-302d-561b-bae6-b88a881d68f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171719 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5 \\\\  & =-2-(-7) \\\\  & =3-(-2) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-7+(n-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-7+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{5n-12 \\mid \\:  n \\in \\mathbb{N}  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: B \\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $-7$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $8$ | $\\cdots$ | $5n-12$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in B,$ we have $f(n) = 5n-12 \\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A| = |B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171744 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171744 #edb/ref 1071 "Do the following sets have the same cardinality? If so, why?\n\n$$\nA = \\mathbb{N}, \\qquad B = \\left\\{1,\\, \\dfrac12,\\,  \\dfrac14,\\,  \\dfrac18,\\,  \\ldots \\right \\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171744 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f47fd84-fda1-5eba-b32e-53b07a391b3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171744 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\dfrac12{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =1\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{  \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\,  \\Bigg |\\:  n \\in \\mathbb{N}  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1}$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in A,$ we have $f(n) =   \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A| = |B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171919 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171919 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171919 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171919 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172389 #edb/ref 17592186