ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171921 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171921 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171921 #edb/ref 1039 "Using Geometric Sequences to Construct a Bijection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171921 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171921 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c0445833-499f-577f-8ca7-265f72d24b77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172199 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172199 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172199 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172199 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172199 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172199 #edb/ref 1045 "Discrete Infinite Sets With Equal Cardinality" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172199 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172199 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172199 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172199 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8a73ceeb-8937-5609-a6e7-41612c249289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172390 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172390 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172390 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA={3,6,12,24,…},\\,B=N\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172390 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5b3d3fb-177d-5a6c-bcc7-9d4791b3f5d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172390 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA={3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}∣\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:B\\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $3$ | $6$ | $12$ | $24$ | $⋯$ | $3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in B,$ we have $f(n)=3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}\\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172690 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172690 #edb/ref 1061 146473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172690 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99a3bd0e-6210-52db-a6b7-e78f36de1139" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172690 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172691 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172691 #edb/ref 1061 146474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172691 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e8a779b4-fed1-5e02-a9a4-d798f96442c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172691 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172692 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172692 #edb/ref 1061 146478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172692 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40ef60e8-6dd4-5f9a-820a-bc636b0e9421" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172692 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172693 #edb/ref 1061 146481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172693 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3945b86a-e5c4-5ef2-b752-d02a16d1d0a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172693 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173263 #edb/ref 1064 "We recently defined two *finite* sets $A$ and $B$ as having the *same cardinality* if they contain the same number of elements. \n\nWhile this might seem a reasonable definition, we'd like to extend the concept of cardinality to infinite sets. Unfortunately, this definition will not do since counting the elements of an infinite set is impossible. \n\nTo get around this difficulty, we need a revised definition. Here it is:\n\n*Two set $A$ and $B$ have the **same cardinality** if there exists a bijection of $A$ onto $B.$ In other words, there is a one-to-one correspondence between the elements of the sets. Otherwise, if no such bijection exists, the sets are said to have **unequal cardinalities**.*\n\nAs before, we write $|A|= |B|$ when $A$ and $B$ have equal cardinalities, and $|A|\\neq |B|$ otherwise.\n\nFor finite sets, the situation is pretty simple. If two sets $A$ and $B$ have equal cardinality, then there exists a mapping that uniquely pairs each element of $A$ with an element of $B.$ This is equivalent to saying that $A$ and $B$ contain the same number of elements. \n\nFor example, the sets $A = \\{ a,b,c, d \\}$ and $B = \\{ 0, 1, 2, 3 \\}$ each have a cardinality of $4.$ The corresponding bijection might be the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d8d2c9de97d811be0e38ec1512546a399ebc3ee299934746a8d47bf30354b7b1.png)\n\n   \n\nIn contrast, for the sets $C = \\{ \\triangle \\}$ and $D = \\{ \\clubsuit, \\spadesuit \\},$ no bijections exist between these sets since they have different numbers of elements. Therefore, they have unequal cardinalities.\n\nFor infinite sets, it's a bit more complicated. However, we have the following fact to get us started:\n\n*If a set $A$ is finite and another set $B$ is infinite, then these sets have unequal cardinalities.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173263 #edb/ref 1065 8225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173263 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "aa23838f-1c70-54b6-a3f5-d7b92a50ad44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173263 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414228 #edb/ref 1080 "differentiable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414228 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414228 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01b4ed91-d3e3-58d2-abd5-d087fd64c99c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414229 #edb/ref 1080 "integrable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414229 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414229 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0299bb8a-9915-5ed0-b427-9ea16c7bfd20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414230 #edb/ref 1080 "|A|\\ne |B|" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414230 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414230 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03306edf-541c-55bb-bdc4-0c703c1cce39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414231 #edb/ref 1080 "injective" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414231 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414231 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03ddee33-7744-5ed1-9e92-d7aa8dfc6ad3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414232 #edb/ref 1080 "injective" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414232 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414232 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09b85ac8-c2b1-53d7-a521-87ffe25b001f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414233 #edb/ref 1080 "{6}^{n}+1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414233 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414233 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e48d6d5-